1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,996
The Marquis and the Museum:

2
00:00:03,076 --> 00:00:07,975
Tokugawa Yoshichika in
Syonan, 1942 to 1944

3
00:00:07,975 --> 00:00:12,172
On the 5th of September 1942
the Syonan Times reported

4
00:00:12,252 --> 00:00:16,687
that Marquis Yoshichika
Tokugawa, adviser to the Japanese

5
00:00:16,767 --> 00:00:21,201
military administration in
Malaya, had been made director

6
00:00:21,281 --> 00:00:25,400
of the Syonan Museum,
Botanical Gardens and Library.

7
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:28,247
These were the old Raffles Museum and

8
00:00:28,327 --> 00:00:31,175
the Botanic Gardens under new names.

9
00:00:31,175 --> 00:00:34,108
The paper added that
"no damage was caused"

10
00:00:34,188 --> 00:00:36,850
to them in the fighting for Singapore.

11
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:40,164
For the next two years
a Japanese nobleman who

12
00:00:40,244 --> 00:00:44,001
had once come to Malaya to
hunt tigers presided over

13
00:00:44,081 --> 00:00:47,475
the island's two great
scientific collections.

14
00:00:47,475 --> 00:00:51,068
Working under him, and
outside the internment camps,

15
00:00:51,148 --> 00:00:55,175
were two British botanists,
Eric Holttum and John Corner.

16
00:00:55,175 --> 00:00:58,739
Both collections came through
the war largely intact,

17
00:00:58,819 --> 00:01:02,325
and their descendants are
still in Singapore today.

18
00:01:02,325 --> 00:01:07,425
Singapore surrendered on
the 15th of February 1942.

19
00:01:07,425 --> 00:01:10,418
Within days a Japanese volcanologist,

20
00:01:10,498 --> 00:01:15,234
Professor Hidezō Tanakadate
of Tohoku Imperial University,

21
00:01:15,314 --> 00:01:19,300
was going round the city's
scientific buildings.

22
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:22,320
He had come down from
Saigon behind the army,

23
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:27,004
and according to later accounts he
used the rank of a junior officer

24
00:01:27,084 --> 00:01:30,793
to post handwritten notices
on the doors of the Raffles

25
00:01:30,873 --> 00:01:34,800
Museum and other buildings,
telling soldiers to keep out.

26
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:38,679
The Botanic Gardens' own
report on the war years,

27
00:01:38,759 --> 00:01:43,446
published in 1947, says that
"three days after the Japanese

28
00:01:43,526 --> 00:01:48,050
occupation" Tanakadate "assumed
control of the Gardens".

29
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:52,556
He asked Eric Holttum, the
Gardens' director since 1925,

30
00:01:52,636 --> 00:01:57,550
to stay in executive charge and
to carry on the normal work.

31
00:01:57,550 --> 00:02:02,006
He then joined the Gardens and
the Raffles Museum into a single

32
00:02:02,086 --> 00:02:07,125
department under the municipality,
with its head office at the museum.

33
00:02:07,125 --> 00:02:11,225
By June 1942 the Syonan Times
was describing his work there:

34
00:02:11,305 --> 00:02:14,986
the museum and the gardens
had been open to the public

35
00:02:15,066 --> 00:02:18,330
since the 29th of April,
the Emperor's birthday,

36
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:22,789
and thousands of rare exhibits
had escaped "the wave of looting"

37
00:02:22,869 --> 00:02:26,700
after the fall of Singapore
thanks to his quick action.

38
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:30,568
Rain coming through the
damaged roof, the paper noted,

39
00:02:30,648 --> 00:02:34,450
had given the museum's
elephant "a jolly good bath".

40
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:39,389
The man who took over from him
in September was a marquis,

41
00:02:39,469 --> 00:02:42,325
and in 1942 that was a real rank.

42
00:02:42,325 --> 00:02:46,282
In 1884 Meiji Japan created
a new peerage, the kazoku,

43
00:02:46,362 --> 00:02:50,544
with five ranks: kōshaku
(prince or duke), kōshaku again,

44
00:02:50,624 --> 00:02:53,909
written with a different
character (marquis),

45
00:02:53,989 --> 00:02:58,475
hakushaku (count), shishaku
(viscount) and danshaku (baron).

46
00:02:58,475 --> 00:03:02,299
The names came from the ranks
of ancient Chinese nobility,

47
00:03:02,379 --> 00:03:04,655
and their official English versions

48
00:03:04,735 --> 00:03:08,100
were the European titles
they were meant to match.

49
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:10,876
The peerage lasted until 1947,

50
00:03:10,956 --> 00:03:15,050
when Japan's new
constitution abolished it.

51
00:03:15,050 --> 00:03:18,362
Yoshichika was born in Tokyo in 1886,

52
00:03:18,442 --> 00:03:24,125
the youngest son of Matsudaira
Shungaku, a former feudal lord.

53
00:03:24,125 --> 00:03:29,755
In 1908 he was adopted into the
Owari branch of the Tokugawa family,

54
00:03:29,835 --> 00:03:33,450
one of the three senior
branches of the clan

55
00:03:33,530 --> 00:03:38,400
that had ruled Japan until
1868, and became its 19th head.

56
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:44,519
He studied history at Tokyo Imperial
University and then turned to biology,

57
00:03:44,599 --> 00:03:49,475
and in 1918 he founded his own
research institute in Tokyo.

58
00:03:49,475 --> 00:03:51,950
He also knew Malaya.

59
00:03:51,950 --> 00:03:56,024
In 1921 he sailed to the
Malay Peninsula and Java,

60
00:03:56,104 --> 00:04:01,176
arriving in Singapore on the
21st of May, and in early June he

61
00:04:01,256 --> 00:04:06,075
went tiger hunting near Muar
with Sultan Ibrahim of Johor.

62
00:04:06,075 --> 00:04:09,755
The trip earned him the
nickname toragari no tonosama,

63
00:04:09,835 --> 00:04:12,819
"the tiger-hunting
lord", and the friendship

64
00:04:12,899 --> 00:04:16,450
with the Sultan lasted for
the rest of their lives.

65
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:20,275
After a later visit he
began to learn Malay.

66
00:04:20,275 --> 00:04:25,875
At home he gave much of his family's
art collection to a foundation,

67
00:04:25,955 --> 00:04:30,550
which opened the Tokugawa
Art Museum in Nagoya in 1935.

68
00:04:30,550 --> 00:04:34,427
His politics were less
settled: in 1931 he gave money

69
00:04:34,507 --> 00:04:38,385
to army officers plotting
a coup, the March Incident,

70
00:04:38,465 --> 00:04:41,825
and then helped persuade
them to call it off.

71
00:04:41,825 --> 00:04:45,052
He left Japan on the
11th of February 1942,

72
00:04:45,132 --> 00:04:49,742
four days before the surrender,
and reached Syonan on the 5th

73
00:04:49,822 --> 00:04:53,975
of March as an adviser to
the military administration.

74
00:04:53,975 --> 00:04:58,000
The Syonan Times report of
the 5th of September 1942

75
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:02,184
said that the institutions
had been "greatly enlarged

76
00:05:02,264 --> 00:05:05,736
under the direction of
Dr. Hideso Tanakadate,

77
00:05:05,816 --> 00:05:10,000
who will continue his duties
under Marquis Tokugawa".

78
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,709
The Gardens' 1947 report
describes the Marquis's position

79
00:05:14,789 --> 00:05:18,742
as Honorary President of
the Museum and Gardens,

80
00:05:18,822 --> 00:05:24,200
which he held until he went back
to Japan in the middle of 1944.

81
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:31,270
In December 1942 Professor Kwan Koriba, a
botanist from Kyoto Imperial University,

82
00:05:31,350 --> 00:05:34,750
arrived to run the Gardens as director.

83
00:05:34,750 --> 00:05:38,131
Holttum and Corner were
among the very few British

84
00:05:38,211 --> 00:05:41,325
civilians in Singapore
who were not interned.

85
00:05:41,325 --> 00:05:45,827
Corner, the Gardens' assistant
director, had a right arm weakened

86
00:05:45,907 --> 00:05:49,925
by a monkey bite and had not
joined the volunteer forces.

87
00:05:49,925 --> 00:05:54,761
The 1947 report says that Koriba
"obtained permission" for both

88
00:05:54,841 --> 00:05:59,600
men to stay in the Gardens and
continue their botanical work.

89
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:03,275
Corner studied the larger
fungi and the development

90
00:06:03,355 --> 00:06:06,300
of flowers and fruits in tropical trees.

91
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:10,568
Holttum wrote new descriptions
of the peninsula's ferns,

92
00:06:10,648 --> 00:06:12,900
orchids, gingers and grasses.

93
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:17,185
Corner and Tanakadate, meanwhile,
drew up lists of abandoned

94
00:06:17,265 --> 00:06:21,986
libraries in government offices
and private homes, and their books

95
00:06:22,066 --> 00:06:26,787
were gathered into the museum, and
Raffles's statue was taken down

96
00:06:26,867 --> 00:06:31,450
from Empress Place in September
1942 and moved into the museum.

97
00:06:31,450 --> 00:06:35,750
Domei said it would be kept
there "as a mark of respect".

98
00:06:35,750 --> 00:06:39,075
The Marquis also spent his own money.

99
00:06:39,075 --> 00:06:43,365
In December 1942 the Syonan
Sinbun reported that he would

100
00:06:43,445 --> 00:06:47,428
pay for five young people
from Johor, three young men

101
00:06:47,508 --> 00:06:51,725
and two young women, to study
in Tokyo for three years.

102
00:06:51,725 --> 00:06:55,711
In Tokyo they lived at his
mansion, and one of them

103
00:06:55,791 --> 00:07:00,175
was Ungku Aziz, later a
well-known Malaysian economist.

104
00:07:00,175 --> 00:07:04,975
One of the stranger results
came to light in 1946.

105
00:07:04,975 --> 00:07:09,412
When the British returned they
found 280 copies of a new book

106
00:07:09,492 --> 00:07:14,965
at the Raffles Museum: C. F. Symington's
Foresters' Manual of Dipterocarps,

107
00:07:15,045 --> 00:07:18,519
a guide to Malaya's most
important timber trees,

108
00:07:18,599 --> 00:07:22,375
written by a British forest
officer before the war.

109
00:07:22,375 --> 00:07:26,024
Symington's whereabouts
were unknown, but in 1942

110
00:07:26,104 --> 00:07:30,057
Tanakadate had a British
forest officer, H. E. Desch,

111
00:07:30,137 --> 00:07:34,471
released from Changi for a
time and taken to Kuala Lumpur,

112
00:07:34,551 --> 00:07:37,975
where a galley proof of
the manual was found.

113
00:07:37,975 --> 00:07:41,338
The printing costs, the
Straits Times reported,

114
00:07:41,418 --> 00:07:44,709
were borne by Tanakadate
and Marquis Tokugawa,

115
00:07:44,789 --> 00:07:48,525
"who acted as President of
the Museum and Library".

116
00:07:48,525 --> 00:07:53,141
Koriba and a Japanese biologist,
Yata Haneda, took the proofs into

117
00:07:53,221 --> 00:07:57,775
the internment camp themselves
so that Desch could correct them.

118
00:07:57,775 --> 00:08:01,294
The book came out at the
end of 1943 under the

119
00:08:01,374 --> 00:08:05,600
museum's own imprint, in the
middle of the occupation.

120
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:10,075
The museum under its new
masters had its own purpose.

121
00:08:10,075 --> 00:08:15,051
In January 1943 the Syonan Sinbun
reported that about 600 people

122
00:08:15,131 --> 00:08:19,395
a day were visiting it, most
of them Japanese soldiers,

123
00:08:19,475 --> 00:08:25,400
and that its staff were translating
every label from English into Japanese.

124
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:28,464
"We want to do away
with English as much as

125
00:08:28,544 --> 00:08:32,200
possible," the curator,
Mr Nakata, told the paper.

126
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:35,948
Corner's own report on the
museum staff, written at the

127
00:08:36,028 --> 00:08:39,776
end of the war, praises one
staff member, Mrs E. Reyes,

128
00:08:39,856 --> 00:08:43,256
who kept trying to stop
others from stealing books

129
00:08:43,336 --> 00:08:47,025
from the library and selling
them in Bras Basah Road.

130
00:08:47,025 --> 00:08:52,100
The Gardens also paid a price that
the scientists could not prevent.

131
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:57,327
According to the 1947 report, more
than half of the outdoor staff,

132
00:08:57,407 --> 00:09:03,519
49 men, were sent by the Japanese military
to work on the Siam–Burma railway,

133
00:09:03,599 --> 00:09:07,700
and 22 of them died there,
both Indians and Malays.

134
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:12,019
Others were set to growing
medicinal, fibre and dye plants on

135
00:09:12,099 --> 00:09:16,425
the neighbouring Tyersall estate,
and later to growing food.

136
00:09:16,425 --> 00:09:21,363
By the end of the occupation
prices had risen to about 500

137
00:09:21,443 --> 00:09:26,375
times their 1941 level, while
wages had not even doubled.

138
00:09:26,375 --> 00:09:29,035
For Holttum and Corner the arrangement

139
00:09:29,115 --> 00:09:31,350
had a cost of a different kind.

140
00:09:31,350 --> 00:09:35,425
Corner signed an undertaking
to the Japanese authorities,

141
00:09:35,505 --> 00:09:40,025
and some of his fellow Britons
regarded him as a collaborator.

142
00:09:40,025 --> 00:09:43,864
The two men's own account, in
the 1947 report, sets out the

143
00:09:43,944 --> 00:09:47,584
basis on which they worked:
"All the Japanese scientific

144
00:09:47,664 --> 00:09:51,304
officers concerned were
insistent that the Herbarium and

145
00:09:51,384 --> 00:09:55,422
library (as also the collections
at the Raffles Museum) should

146
00:09:55,502 --> 00:09:59,142
be preserved as part of the
cultural heritage of Malaya,

147
00:09:59,222 --> 00:10:03,075
and on this basis the British
staff cooperated with them."

148
00:10:03,075 --> 00:10:06,785
The Marquis left Syonan in August 1944,

149
00:10:06,865 --> 00:10:09,975
under orders to return to Japan.

150
00:10:09,975 --> 00:10:13,395
His diary from that
month has a short line:

151
00:10:13,475 --> 00:10:16,975
Marē ga suki na no de
aru, "I love Malaya."

152
00:10:16,975 --> 00:10:20,323
Tanakadate had already been recalled,

153
00:10:20,403 --> 00:10:24,769
in 1943, and his own books
and research notes in

154
00:10:24,849 --> 00:10:28,925
Tokyo were destroyed
in an air raid in 1945.

155
00:10:28,925 --> 00:10:32,375
Koriba stayed in Singapore to the end.

156
00:10:32,375 --> 00:10:36,552
When the war was over, Holttum and
Corner were given permission to

157
00:10:36,632 --> 00:10:41,132
have him released from the camp for
Japanese prisoners, but he declined

158
00:10:41,212 --> 00:10:45,275
and stayed with his countrymen,
a choice Holttum later praised.

159
00:10:45,275 --> 00:10:50,048
The 1947 report found that the
herbarium had survived intact,

160
00:10:50,128 --> 00:10:53,151
apart from some specimens that had been

161
00:10:53,231 --> 00:10:57,050
on loan in Germany, and
that so had the library.

162
00:10:57,050 --> 00:11:01,753
Under the British Military
Administration the Gardens were

163
00:11:01,833 --> 00:11:07,609
looked after until March 1946 by
Gilbert Archey of the Auckland Museum,

164
00:11:07,689 --> 00:11:11,400
and Holttum returned as
director in May 1946.

165
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:14,560
In Japan the Marquis lost most of

166
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:18,175
his fortune and, in 1947, his title.

167
00:11:18,175 --> 00:11:22,256
He became an adviser to
the new Japan Socialist

168
00:11:22,336 --> 00:11:26,949
Party in 1945, was purged
from public office in 1946,

169
00:11:27,029 --> 00:11:30,925
and spent his last years
in charitable work.

170
00:11:30,925 --> 00:11:34,725
He died in Tokyo in 1976.

171
00:11:34,725 --> 00:11:38,001
Five years later Corner
published The Marquis:

172
00:11:38,081 --> 00:11:41,431
A Tale of Syonan-to, a
memoir of the occupation

173
00:11:41,511 --> 00:11:44,575
years and of his friendship with Tokugawa.

174
00:11:44,575 --> 00:11:48,165
The Straits Times reviewed
it in February 1982

175
00:11:48,245 --> 00:11:51,675
under the headline "A
wartime controversy".

176
00:11:51,675 --> 00:11:55,101
The old Raffles Museum
building on Stamford

177
00:11:55,181 --> 00:11:58,850
Road is now the National
Museum of Singapore.

178
00:11:58,850 --> 00:12:01,637
Its natural history collections,

179
00:12:01,717 --> 00:12:07,997
the specimens Tanakadate's notices
protected in 1942, were moved to the

180
00:12:08,077 --> 00:12:12,825
University of Singapore's
zoology department in 1972.

181
00:12:12,825 --> 00:12:17,438
They spent years in temporary
homes, including seven years at

182
00:12:17,518 --> 00:12:22,747
Nanyang Technological University,
and in 2015 they moved into the new

183
00:12:22,827 --> 00:12:28,748
Lee Kong Chian Natural History Museum
at the National University of Singapore,

184
00:12:28,828 --> 00:12:32,675
which now holds more than
560,000 catalogued lots.

185
00:12:32,675 --> 00:12:36,510
The Botanic Gardens
became Singapore's first

186
00:12:36,590 --> 00:12:40,150
UNESCO World Heritage Site in July 2015.

187
00:12:40,150 --> 00:12:44,545
The building that held Holttum's
office and laboratory as director

188
00:12:44,625 --> 00:12:48,478
is now Holttum Hall, home of
the Gardens' heritage museum,

189
00:12:48,558 --> 00:12:53,575
and Corner's old house in the Gardens
is a restaurant called Corner House.

190
00:12:53,575 --> 00:12:56,027
In Nagoya the Tokugawa Art Museum,

191
00:12:56,107 --> 00:13:00,725
which the Marquis founded, still
shows the family's treasures.

192
00:13:00,725 --> 00:13:04,330
Where it fits in the bigger
story: the occupation took

193
00:13:04,410 --> 00:13:06,718
a heavy toll on Singapore's people,

194
00:13:06,798 --> 00:13:10,881
including the Gardens' own
workers on the Siam–Burma railway,

195
00:13:10,961 --> 00:13:14,850
and the museum was put to work
for Japan's cultural aims.

196
00:13:14,850 --> 00:13:18,500
Yet the island's oldest
scientific collections came

197
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:22,889
through it largely intact,
because a Japanese volcanologist,

198
00:13:22,969 --> 00:13:26,107
a Japanese marquis and
two British botanists

199
00:13:26,187 --> 00:13:29,625
agreed that they belonged
to Malaya's heritage.

200
00:13:29,625 --> 00:13:32,643
Most visitors to the
Botanic Gardens and the

201
00:13:32,723 --> 00:13:37,501
natural history museum today know
little of that wartime arrangement,

202
00:13:37,581 --> 00:13:39,200
which is still debated.
